7.5.m:
Going there, they embrace it, and it too is an exceedingly dreadful fire; / thus they are tormented day and night in that terrible place, even upon the heights.
Hear from me which men go helpless to that place: / he who approaches the wife of a guru, or who steals the property of a guru;
and he who eats the property of a deity, or repeatedly destroys it; / and he who steals the property of a brahmin, or withholds it by force; // / and he who steals the property of a child, or steals the wealth of a woman; / and he who abandons eunuchs, and he who slays one who has sought refuge;
and he who covets his wealth; and he who steals the property of a deity; / he who eats everything indiscriminately, and he who sells water.
And whoever is a seller of Soma, and whoever extinguishes sacred fires, / and whoever strikes with fire, and whoever strikes with poison; / and that man who slays in battle an unarmed person with a weapon, / or one who is begging for mercy, or even one who is seated;

Moreover, opening their mouths, Yama's cruel servants / pour iron liquefied by fearful fire. / Likewise, women and men made of iron, radiant as fire, / unite and embrace them as they flee this way and that.
And there are many trees there, bearing fine flowers, fruits, and leaves; / afflicted with agony, they seek shelter under them, but those [trees] also rain down fire. / And there are many other rocks there; whenever they embrace them, / those rocks, shining like fire, burn those men severely.
In this way has this hell, Mahāpadma, been described. / A man who steals gold, and who approaches his mother [sexually],
and the son who approaches his mother; one who violates celibacy and defiles the Veda; / one who is envious, vicious, and a worshipper of śramaṇas;
a śūdra who recites or even listens to the Śruti, / or the foolish-minded one who accepts [its meaning], or one who despises brahmins;
